# HED-FLORAL-01 — Rose de Mai Unadorned — Deep-Plane Perfume — 30 mL — v5

**Date:** 2026-09-02  
**Claim mode:** unclaimed  
**Family archetype:** `floral_soliflore.rose_reference`  
**Immediate parent:** `formulas/HED-FLORAL-01_Rose_de_Mai_Unadorned_DeepPlane_FINAL_30mL_v4.md`  
**Parent formula-definition SHA-256:** `596e86bf80f587d8835ebe92a135a8426de19d8acf4f1d4787030dc02a1dd256`  
**Physical Rose constraint:** exactly 3,000 uL of `Rose de Mai Absolute (10% in DPG)`  
**Essential oils:** NONE  
**Citronellol:** PROHIBITED  
**Status:** FINAL MULTI-MATERIAL COMPUTATIONAL DESIGN — 0 HARD GATE FAILURES — SENSORY / HEDONIC / SAFETY / STABILITY / PHYSICAL PERFORMANCE NOT TESTED — RELEASE HOLD

## Design Correction

Version 4 over-literalized fingerprint preservation and collapsed the requested perfume into a Rose-stock dilution. Version 5 restores actual perfumery while keeping the correction that matters: no added Citronellol and no generic Rose-alcohol reconstruction.

The Rose de Mai stock remains the only floral subject. Supporting materials are assigned distinct subordinate functions: petal continuity, aperture, light-to-warm adhesion, low-character fixation, a single transparent projection veil, and a trace waxy-floral tail. Each has an explicit takeover ceiling and may be removed after the modeled and omission audits.

## Final Formula

| # | Ingredient | Stock | Raw amount (uL) | Nominal active (uL) | Role and takeover ceiling |
|---:|---|---|---:|---:|---|
| 1 | Rose de Mai Absolute | 10% in DPG | 3000 | 300 | Protected subject; its natural petal, honey, green, and residual facets must remain recognizable throughout |
| 2 | Phenethyl Alcohol | neat | 60 | 60 | Petal continuity at 40% of the v3 dose; reject any Rose-water flattening |
| 3 | Hedione | neat | 40 | 40 | Controlled aperture and diffusion; reject an identifiable jasmine halo |
| 4 | Hexyl Salicylate | neat | 40 | 40 | Sheer green-floral adhesion; reject a persistent salicylate film |
| 5 | Benzyl Salicylate | neat | 60 | 60 | Warm lower-petal cushion and diffusion; capped to avoid waxy cosmetic takeover |
| 6 | Benzyl Benzoate | neat | 100 | 100 | Low-character fixation and mass; reject excessive heaviness or slowed bloom |
| 7 | Romandolide | neat | 10 | 10 | Sole musk: transparent outward diffusion at the raw-dose floor; reject clean woody-musk separation |
| 8 | Farnesol | neat | 10 | 10 | Waxy-floral tail at the raw-dose floor; reject muguet or creamy-wax takeover |
|  | **Aromatic stock charge total** |  | **3320** | **620** |  |

### Finished 30 mL spray

- Aromatic stock charge: 3,320 uL.
- Ethanol 96%: 26,680 uL.
- Finished total: 30,000 uL / 30.000 mL.
- Rose stock: exactly 3,000 uL.
- Nominal Rose active: 300 uL / 10,000 ppm v/v proxy in the finished spray.
- Nominal DPG inherited from the Rose stock: approximately 2,700 uL / 9.00% v/v proxy.
- Added Citronellol, Geraniol, Nerol, Rhodinol, Rose Oxide, or damascone materials: none.
- Essential oils: none.

Exact active ppm w/w is unavailable because the Rose stock fraction basis and complete density chain are not authoritative.

## Deep-Plane Architecture

| Plane | v5 expression | Failure detector |
|---|---|---|
| Identity | Rose de Mai remains the sole named flower and dominant recognizer. | Generic Rose accord, Rose water, jasmine, magnolia, violet, cosmetic cream, or musk becomes the subject. |
| Morphology | Living petal core, intrinsic honey/nectar, damp-green edge, transparent lower-petal cushion, and a faint waxy natural residue. | Checklist facets separate into individually identifiable materials. |
| Function | Every support has one bounded role; Rose supplies the flower itself. | Any support duplicates Rose-alcohol character or lacks an omission loss. |
| Relation | PEA continues petal; Hedione opens space; salicylates form a light-to-warm adhesion gradient; Benzyl Benzoate fixes; Romandolide gives a narrow outward veil; Farnesol closes the petal with a trace waxy tail. | Relation becomes takeover, redundancy, masking, or a second floral subject. |
| Mixture | Supports are intentionally subdominant in identity even when perceptible. | Modeled or observed support dominance is treated as acceptable merely because Rose remains the largest dose. |
| Temporal | Rose lift to petal heart to Rose-led warm residue, with no synthetic handoff replacing the flower. | Opening becomes Hedione/PEA, heart becomes salicylate cosmetic, or drydown becomes musk/ionone. |
| Spatial | Focused Rose bloom with controlled air, soft surface, and intimate trail. | Broad white-floral halo or anonymous musk cloud. |
| Physicochemical | Exact raw-volume arithmetic, live stock forms, composite-natural OAV, and time screening. | Monomolecular natural OAV, missing carrier displacement, or exact ppm w/w claimed without basis. |
| Biological/sensitivity | No receptor, universal response, or anosmia claim. | Mechanism or population claims inferred from ingredient identity. |
| Hedonic | Seek fidelity, pleasure, dimensionality, coherence, and comfort separately. | A generic scalar score is called best-smelling evidence. |
| Inventory/build | All materials must resolve to live stock; no EO or Citronellol rows. | Alias ambiguity, depleted stock, unresolved preparation, or sub-10-uL raw dose. |
| Evidence/authority | User correction controls Citronellol exclusion; models remain diagnostic. | OAV, availability, material count, or conventionality overrides target fit. |
| Experiment | Omission hierarchy is Rose-only control, then remove Nerolidol, Farnesol, musk, Benzyl Salicylate, Hedione, PEA, and Hexyl Salicylate individually if a defect appears. | Bundled changes prevent attribution or scarce Rose stock is consumed without authorization. |

## Final Exclusions

- Citronellol, Geraniol, Nerol, and Rhodinol are excluded because they directly rebalance the Rose-alcohol fingerprint.
- Hydroxycitronellol and PEDMC are excluded despite their floral compatibility; their clean peony/magnolia texture would introduce another flower identity.
- Rose Oxide and damascone materials are excluded because their recognizers are too powerful for the current working-stock floor.
- Benzyl Salicylate is deliberately limited to 60 uL and paired with lighter Hexyl Salicylate; it is not used as a heavy white-floral base.
- Only one musk is used. Romandolide is selected at the 10 uL raw floor because its modeled OAV is perceptible enough to supply outward diffusion without the larger creamy-lactonic mass of Ethylene Brassylate. Ethylene Brassylate is also excluded because its current dose receipt lacks a bound inventory source row. Ambrettolide remains preparation-blocked; Habanolide and Exaltolide are sub-threshold at the minimum executable dose; Galaxolide and Zenolide would pull toward laundry or cool-clean musk.
- The unresolved 0.1% Beta Ionone stock and the inventory-unresolved PEDMC identity from the rejected first candidate are not forced through aliases; both are removed.
- Dihydrojasmone and the 50% Benzyl Alcohol row from the rejected second candidate are removed: both failed live stock resolution, and their modeled OAV channels were too prominent for fingerprint-preserving support.
- Phenyl Ethyl Acetate from the rejected third candidate is removed because its stock did not resolve through the authoritative snapshot and its minimum-dose modeled channel was too prominent.
- Nerolidol is removed from the passing nine-row screen because its 10 uL raw-floor dose modeled at OAV 0.02. That cannot honestly support a perceptible balmy-floral role; material count alone is not depth.

## Claim Ceiling

This is the final computational design for the available 3,000 uL Rose stock. Its parser, live inventory contract, composite-OAV screen, temporal screen, and G15 parent comparison have no hard failures. It is not authorized for compounding or release because sensory, safety, stability, and physical-performance evidence are not measured, and it is not empirically proven best-smelling.

